The tests need to use an in-memory DuckDB database to avoid needing the real .duckdb file. Here's a comprehensive test file:


Created a file, read a file
Created a file, read a file
Here's what the test file covers:

Setup — openTestDB creates an in-memory DuckDB (empty string path) and seeds it with 8 movies, 5 actors, 8 genre rows, and 12 role rows that mirror the real schema exactly.

Test categories:

Category	Tests
Query struct	Fields populate correctly, queries slice is non-empty, every entry has a title/SQL/columns
DB connectivity	Ping succeeds, invalid path returns an error
Schema / seed	All four tables exist, row counts match seed data
runQuery	Happy path, invalid SQL returns error, column name override, column count mismatch (graceful), NULL values, empty result set
Production queries	All four run without error against the test DB
Query 1 SQL	Drama ranks above Action, correct column count
Query 2 SQL	Alice Smith (4 movies) is the top actor, all gender values are M/F
Query 3 SQL	Results have 3 columns, rows are year-descending
Query 4 SQL	4 columns returned, male + female ≤ total holds for every genre
DB_PATH env	Default constant is non-empty, env override is respected
To run:

bash
go test ./...               # all tests
go test -v ./...            # verbose
go test -run TestRunQuery   # specific group
The tests are self-contained — no .duckdb file needed, no external fixtures.


